    "question": {
        "name": "CSEC Phy Jan 2023 Q1b(ii)/c/d — Pendulum T² Table, Gradient, and g",
        "text": "<p>Table 1 shows the results of a simple pendulum experiment:</p><table><thead><tr><th>Length l (m)</th><th>Period T (s)</th><th>Period² T² (s²)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>0.15</td><td>0.77</td><td>?</td></tr><tr><td>0.25</td><td>0.99</td><td>?</td></tr><tr><td>0.35</td><td>1.15</td><td>?</td></tr><tr><td>0.45</td><td>1.34</td><td>?</td></tr><tr><td>0.55</td><td>1.48</td><td>?</td></tr><tr><td>0.65</td><td>1.62</td><td>?</td></tr><tr><td>0.75</td><td>1.75</td><td>?</td></tr></tbody></table><p><strong>(b)(ii)</strong> Complete Table 1 by inserting the T² values. <em>(4 marks)</em></p><p><strong>(c)</strong> Calculate the gradient S of the T² vs l graph. <em>(6 marks)</em></p><p><strong>(d)</strong> Given that S = 4π²/g, calculate the acceleration due to gravity, g. <em>(4 marks)</em></p>",
        "general_feedback": "<p><strong>Q1b(ii) — T² values (to 2 d.p.):</strong><br>0.77² = 0.59 | 0.99² = 0.98 | 1.15² = 1.32 | 1.34² = 1.80 | 1.48² = 2.19 | 1.62² = 2.62 | 1.75² = 3.06</p><p><strong>Q1c — Gradient S:</strong><br>Using well-spaced points from the best-fit line (e.g. (0.15, 0.59) and (0.75, 3.06)):<br>S = ΔT²/Δl = (3.06 − 0.59) / (0.75 − 0.15) = 2.47 / 0.60 ≈ <strong>4.12 s² m⁻¹</strong></p><p><strong>Q1d — g from gradient:</strong><br>S = 4π²/g → g = 4π²/S = 39.48 / 4.12 ≈ <strong>9.6 m s⁻²</strong></p><p><em>Note:</em> Accept S between 3.90 and 4.30 s²/m (graph-reading tolerance). Accept g between 9.2 and 10.2 m/s² for own-figure marking.</p>"
    },
